body: "**Found by driving the engine end to end against a real working tree**, not by reading code. Three related defects; the third is what makes the first two dangerous.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```bash\npm vcs init\nprintf \"base\\n\"      > f.txt && pm vcs add && pm vcs commit --message base\npm vcs branch b && pm vcs switch b\nprintf \"from-b\\n\"    > f.txt && pm vcs add && pm vcs commit --message b\npm vcs switch main\nprintf \"from-main\\n\" > f.txt && pm vcs add && pm vcs commit --message m\n\npm vcs status                                   # clean: true\npm vcs merge b --message \"flagged merge\" --fail-on-conflict; echo \"EXIT=$?\"\n```\n\nObserved:\n\n| | expected | actual |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| exit code with `--fail-on-conflict` | non-zero | **0** |\n| commits after the merge | 2 (refused) | **3 — the merge was committed** |\n| `pm vcs status` afterwards | conflicted / unmerged | **`clean: true`** |\n\n## 1. The conflict markers are committed into the tree, not just written to the working file\n\nProven by diffing the merge commit against its first parent:\n\n```\n--- a/doc.txt\n+++ b/doc.txt\n@@ -1,4 +1,10 @@\n line one\n+<<<<<<< ours\n CONFLICT VERSION B\n+||||||| base\n+line two CHANGED ON FEATURE\n+=======\n+CONFLICT VERSION A\n+>>>>>>> theirs\n line three\n```\n\nSo this is not a working-tree artifact awaiting resolution — history permanently contains a commit whose tree has conflict markers in it. Anything that later builds, imports, or bundles that revision gets the markers.\n\n## 2. `--fail-on-conflict` does not exit non-zero\n\nIts help text reads *\"Exit non-zero when the merge leaves any conflict.\"* In the fixture above, on a genuine single-line content conflict, it exits **0**.\n\nI mis-measured this twice before trusting it, and the false readings are worth naming so the flag is not dismissed as working: a second attempt exited 1, but for an unrelated reason — the tree still held unresolved markers, and the merge was refused with *\"The working tree has changes that are not committed\"*. That refusal is correct behaviour and is not this flag firing. The result above is from a fresh repository with `clean: true` immediately before the merge.\n\n## 3. `status` reports clean afterwards — this is the part that makes it unsafe\n\nThe merge command *does* report the problem in its own output: `ok: false`, `clean: false`, and `conflicts: [{path: \"doc.txt\", reason: \"content\"}]`. But that information exists only in the return value of a command that has already finished. Afterwards the repository carries **no state** saying a resolution is owed:\n\n```\npm vcs status\n  clean: true\n```\n\nGit makes conflict *sticky*: `MERGE_HEAD` is set, the index holds unmerged paths, `git status` says \"Unmerged paths\", and `git commit` refuses until they are resolved. Here the conflict is a transient message. An agent that merges, checks `status`, sees clean, and proceeds is behaving correctly against this contract and still ships conflict markers.\n\nThat combination — committed markers, zero exit, clean status — means **nothing durable in the repository records that a merge did not succeed**.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\nRefusing to commit is the behaviour that matches every other refusal in this project: fail closed, leave the caller something to act on. Concretely:\n\n1. A conflicted merge should not produce a commit. Leave the conflicted content in the working tree and record in-progress merge state (the merge target, the bases) in the repository.\n2. `status` should report that state and name the unresolved paths, so the signal survives the command that produced it.\n3. `commit` should refuse while that state exists, with a message naming the paths.\n4. `--fail-on-conflict` then becomes a modifier on an already-safe default rather than the only thing standing between a conflict and history. Given (1)-(3), consider making the safe behaviour the default and keeping a flag for the \"commit the markers anyway\" case, if that case is wanted at all.\n\n## What worked well, for contrast\n\nEverything else in the lifecycle behaved correctly and is worth recording so this reads as one sharp defect rather than general unsoundness:\n\n- `init`, `add`, `commit`, `branch`, `switch`, `log`, `diff` all correct.\n- A genuine **three-way merge** of two non-overlapping edits (one branch changing line two, the other appending line four) produced the correct union with `kind: \"merged\"`, `clean: true`.\n- Conflict rendering is **diff3 style with the base section** (`||||||| base`), which is more informative than the default git two-way form.\n- Merging over a dirty working tree is correctly refused, with an accurate reason.\n\nMinor, separate: revisions must be given in full — a 12-character abbreviation is rejected with *\"is not a known commit, branch, tag or remote-tracking branch\"*. Abbreviated revisions are ubiquitous in every comparable tool and their absence is felt immediately when reading `log` output and trying to use it.\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-08-11T20:24:17Z)\n>\n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-vcs#1 - Add pm-vcs: preflight a checkout, predict a merge's per-field resolution, and map commit ranges onto pm items [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-vcs#2 - Make pm-vcs an actual version control system: object store, refs, index, diff, three-way merge, records, oplog and bundles [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-vcs#3 - Add transactional history rewriting and stable change identities [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-vcs#6 - Track PM-linked files with stable native identities [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-vcs#7 - Warm materialized stat caches without risking staged or untracked work [merged]\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\": \"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
